+# ADR: Preserve current caller identity through HNSW replacements
+
+## Status
+
+Accepted for this implementation; exact-revision independent review and scoped checks are required before publication.
+
+## Context
+
+The public HNSW index accepts repeated caller IDs as updates. Its forward ID map and current vector were replaced, but obsolete reverse mappings remained searchable. Searches could return an ID twice with its old score; deleting that ID could leave its old version searchable. Removed physical graph rows could also crowd live rows out of a requested top-k. Older serialized snapshots retained the same stale reverse mappings.
+
+## Decision
+
+Invalidate the previous reverse mapping on successful single and batch replacement. Keep the underlying graph's allocated rows and existing binary format. Restore only reverse mappings matching the live vector and canonical forward mapping when loading snapshots.
+
+Hold one read lock while searching. Start with the caller's original k/ef behavior and widen candidate count by bounded doubling when historical positions underfill live results, up to the finite allocated graph-position count. Return at most min(k, live count) current IDs. This does not turn approximate nearest-neighbor search into an exact scan or rebuild.
+
+## Tradeoffs
+
+Replacement/deletion-heavy indexes may traverse substantially more historical graph positions, with a correspondingly larger ef. Worst-case candidate expansion approaches the allocated graph size. The unchanged physical graph still consumes memory until reconstruction; this change repairs public identity and underfilled retrieval without adding concurrent graph compaction. The read lock freezes mappings during bounded retries.
+
+## Validation
+
+Seven public Rust regressions cover single/batch replacement, deletion after replacement, top-k underfill, current serialization round-trip, a binary snapshot generated by the original source, and ordinary/empty/dimension-error controls. Original source fails six cases and passes the control. The original snapshot contains changed@1, changed@100 and keeper@2 and returns changed twice; fixture provenance is recorded in tests/fixtures/hnsw-legacy-replacement.md. Scoped fixed tests and broader checks are recorded separately before publication; no cross-platform or hosted-CI claim is made.
